LS(g, "reported-speech", "Reported speech", 28,
   ["Change statements, questions and commands from direct to reported speech.", "Apply the backshift of tenses and the changes of pronouns and time words.", "Use suitable reporting verbs."],
   [tx("**Direct speech** gives the speaker's exact words: *Ada said, \"I am tired.\"* **Reported (indirect) speech** tells what was said without quoting: *Ada said that she was tired.* There are no inverted commas, and the tense, pronouns and time words usually change."),
    ky("definition", "Backshift of tenses",
       "When the reporting verb is in the **past** (*said, told, asked*), the tense moves **one step back**:\n- present simple → past simple: *\"I work\"* → *he said he worked*\n- present continuous → past continuous\n- present perfect and simple past → **past perfect** (*had worked*)\n- will → **would**; can → **could**; may → **might**; must → **had to**.\nIf the statement is a permanent truth, the tense may stay the same."),
    ky("definition", "Other changes",
       "- **Pronouns** follow the sense: *\"I\"* → he/she, *\"my\"* → his/her, *\"we\"* → they.\n- **Time and place:** now → *then*; today → *that day*; tomorrow → *the next day*; yesterday → *the day before*; here → *there*; this → *that*.\n- **Questions:** no question mark, statement order, **if/whether** for yes/no questions: *\"Are you ready?\"* → *She asked if I was ready.*\n- **Commands:** *told / asked + to-infinitive*: *\"Sit down!\"* → *He told us to sit down.*"),
    fg(cols("Tense backshift", ["Direct", "Reported"], [["am / is / are", "was / were"], ["go / goes", "went"], ["have gone / went", "had gone"], ["will / can", "would / could"]], w=430),
       "Shift one step back into the past.", "A table showing backshift: am is are become was were; go goes become went; have gone or went become had gone; will and can become would and could."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- Keeping the question mark and the question order: *She asked where was I* → **She asked where I was.**\n- *He said me that...* → **He told me that...** (told + person) or **He said that...**\n- Forgetting to change time words: *tomorrow* → *the next day*.\n- Keeping *I/my* instead of changing the pronoun.\n- Leaving the inverted commas."),
    exm("Example 1: a statement", "Report: *Ngu said, \"I am going to the market tomorrow.\"*",
        ["I → he; am going → **was going**; tomorrow → **the next day**.", "No inverted commas and add *that* if you like."], "Ngu said that he was going to the market the next day."),
    exm("Example 2: a question", "Report: *The teacher asked, \"Have you finished your homework?\"*",
        ["Yes/no question → *if/whether*; present perfect → **past perfect**; you → **we** or **I** depending on the listener.", "Use statement order and no question mark."], "The teacher asked if we had finished our homework.")],
   [m("Report: *\"I am tired,\" said Ada.*", "Ada said that she was tired.", ["Ada said that she is tired yesterday.", "Ada said that I was tired.", "Ada said am tired."], "Present simple be becomes was and I becomes she."),
    m("Report the question: *\"Where do you live?\" he asked me.*", "He asked me where I lived.", ["He asked me where did I live?", "He asked me where do I live?", "He asked me that where I lived."], "Statement order and backshift: lived."),
    t("In reported speech *tomorrow* usually becomes *the next day*.", True, "It usually changes after a past reporting verb."),
    m("Which is the correct report of *\"Sit down!\"*?", "He told us to sit down.", ["He said us sit down.", "He told us sit down.", "He told us that sit down."], "Command: told + person + to + verb."),
    m("*\"We will visit you,\"* said Mbah. Choose the report.", "Mbah said that they would visit us.", ["Mbah said that we will visit you.", "Mbah said they will visited us.", "Mbah told that they would visit."], "Will becomes would and we becomes they."),
    o("Report each sentence:\n1. \"I have lost my bag,\" said Ndi.\n2. The principal asked, \"Why are you late?\"\n3. \"Don't run in the corridor,\" the prefect told us.\n4. \"I will phone you tomorrow,\" my uncle promised.",
      "1. Ndi said that she/he had lost her/his bag. 2. The principal asked why we were late. 3. The prefect told us not to run in the corridor. 4. My uncle promised that he would phone me the next day.",
      ["1 mark each: correct backshift and pronouns.", "Sentence 2: statement order, no question mark.", "Sentence 3: not to + verb.", "Sentence 4: would, the next day."])],
   [sc("In reported speech after *said*, the present simple usually becomes...", "the past simple", ["the future", "the present perfect", "an infinitive"], "One step back."),
    sc("*Now* in direct speech usually becomes...", "then", ["here", "today", "tomorrow"], "Time shift."),
    sc("A yes/no question in reported speech takes...", "if or whether", ["what", "that", "to"], "If or whether."),
    sc("*Will* becomes...", "would", ["shall", "can", "must"], "Backshift."),
    sc("Which is a reported command?", "He told us to be quiet.", ["He said, \"Be quiet!\"", "Be quiet, he said", "He asked be quiet."], "Told + object + to-infinitive.")])

LS(g, "passive-voice", "The passive voice", 26,
   ["Form the passive in common tenses.", "Decide when the passive is a good choice.", "Change active sentences to passive and back."],
   [tx("In an **active** sentence the subject does the action: *The farmers harvested the cocoa.* In a **passive** sentence the thing that receives the action becomes the subject: *The cocoa was harvested (by the farmers).* We use the passive when the doer is unknown, unimportant, or when we want to put the focus on the receiver."),
    ky("definition", "Forming the passive",
       "**be (in the same tense) + past participle**:\n- present simple: *The letter is written.*\n- present continuous: *The road is being repaired.*\n- simple past: *The match was cancelled.*\n- present perfect: *The window has been broken.*\n- future: *The results will be announced.*\n- modals: *The work must be finished. Water can be purified.*\nThe doer (**agent**) comes after **by** and is often left out."),
    fg(shapes([T(210, 22, "Active to passive: the roles swap", 15, bold=True), T(30, 52, "Active", 13, anchor="start", bold=True), T(30, 152, "Passive", 13, anchor="start", bold=True),
               RECT(20, 62, 120, 34, fill="lightblue", radius=6), T(80, 84, "Subject", 13), RECT(150, 62, 120, 34, fill="lightgreen", radius=6), T(210, 84, "Verb", 13), RECT(280, 62, 120, 34, fill="lightorange", radius=6), T(340, 84, "Object", 13),
               RECT(20, 162, 120, 34, fill="lightorange", radius=6), T(80, 184, "Object", 13), RECT(150, 162, 120, 34, fill="lightgreen", radius=6), T(210, 184, "be + pp", 13), RECT(280, 162, 120, 34, fill="lightblue", radius=6), T(340, 184, "by + Subject", 13),
               LINE(210, 96, 210, 162, arrow="end", color="green", width=2)], 420, 215),
       "The object moves to the front; the verb becomes be + participle.", "Two rows of boxes: the active row has subject, verb and object; the passive row has object, be plus past participle, and by plus subject, with an arrow from the verb to the be plus participle box."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- Forgetting the participle: *The cocoa was harvest* → **harvested**.\n- Wrong tense of *be*: *The road is repaired now* (continuous: **is being repaired**).\n- Only **transitive** verbs (verbs with an object) have a passive: *arrive, happen, die, go* do not (*The accident was happened* is wrong).\n- Writing *by* + a pronoun in the wrong form: *by him / by them*, not *by he*.\n- Overusing the passive in stories: it makes writing heavy."),
    exm("Example 1: active to passive", "Rewrite in the passive: *The workers repaired the bridge last month.*",
        ["Object *the bridge* becomes the subject; simple past → **was repaired**.", "The agent is optional: *by the workers*."], "The bridge was repaired (by the workers) last month."),
    exm("Example 2: passive to active", "Rewrite in the active: *The letters will be delivered by the postman tomorrow.*",
        ["The doer is *the postman*: he becomes the subject.", "Future passive *will be delivered* becomes active *will deliver*."], "The postman will deliver the letters tomorrow.")],
   [m("Which sentence is in the passive?", "The cocoa was harvested last week.", ["The farmers harvested the cocoa.", "The farmers are harvesting.", "The farmers will harvest cocoa."], "Was + past participle."),
    m("Passive of *They are building a school.*", "A school is being built.", ["A school is built.", "A school was building.", "A school has built."], "Present continuous passive: is being + participle."),
    t("Verbs such as *arrive* and *happen* can be put in the passive.", False, "They are intransitive: they take no object, so no passive."),
    m("Complete: *The results ___ tomorrow.*", "will be announced", ["will announced", "are announce", "was announcing"], "Future passive."),
    m("Passive of *Someone has stolen my bicycle.*", "My bicycle has been stolen.", ["My bicycle is stolen.", "My bicycle was been stolen.", "My bicycle has stolen."], "Present perfect passive: has been + participle."),
    o("Rewrite each sentence in the passive:\n1. The teacher marks the exercise books every Friday.\n2. The villagers built a well last year.\n3. The council is repairing the road.\n4. They must finish the work today.",
      "1. The exercise books are marked by the teacher every Friday. 2. A well was built by the villagers last year. 3. The road is being repaired by the council. 4. The work must be finished today.",
      ["1 mark each: correct form of be and past participle.", "The agent may be omitted when unimportant.", "Tenses of the verbs are kept."])],
   [sc("The passive is formed with...", "be + past participle", ["do + verb", "have + -ing", "will + base"], "That is the rule."),
    sc("The doer in a passive sentence is introduced by...", "by", ["with", "to", "of"], "The agent follows by."),
    sc("Which verb cannot be used in the passive?", "arrive", ["build", "write", "repair"], "Intransitive verbs have no passive."),
    sc("*The road is being repaired* is...", "present continuous passive", ["simple past passive", "future passive", "active"], "Is being + participle."),
    sc("We use the passive when...", "the doer is unknown or unimportant", ["we want a question", "we want a command", "we want the future"], "Focus on the receiver.")])

LS(g, "conditionals-relative", "Conditional sentences and relative clauses", 28,
   ["Form zero, first, second and third conditionals.", "Use who, which, whose, that and where in relative clauses.", "Punctuate defining and non-defining clauses."],
   [tx("A **conditional sentence** has an *if* clause (the condition) and a main clause (the result). The tense pattern shows how real or unreal the condition is. A **relative clause** gives more information about a noun."),
    ky("definition", "Four conditionals",
       "- **Zero** (general truth): *If you heat ice, it melts.* (if + present, present)\n- **First** (real, possible): *If it rains, we will stay at home.* (if + present, will + verb)\n- **Second** (unreal or unlikely now): *If I were rich, I would build a hospital.* (if + past, would + verb; use **were** for all persons)\n- **Third** (impossible, past): *If she had studied, she would have passed.* (if + past perfect, would have + participle)\n**Unless** = *if not*: *Unless you hurry, you will be late.*"),
    ky("definition", "Relative clauses",
       "- **who** (people), **which** (things), **whose** (ownership), **where** (places), **that** (people or things, defining only).\n- **Defining clause** (needed to know which one): no commas: *The boy who sits behind me is my cousin.*\n- **Non-defining clause** (extra information): between commas, no *that*: *Mount Cameroon, which is in the South West, is often cloudy.*"),
    fg(cols("Conditional patterns", ["Type", "If clause", "Main clause"], [["first", "present", "will + verb"], ["second", "past", "would + verb"], ["third", "past perfect", "would have + pp"]], w=450),
       "The tense in the if clause moves back as the condition becomes less real.", "A table of conditional patterns: first conditional present then will plus verb; second past then would plus verb; third past perfect then would have plus participle."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- *If it will rain, we will stay* → **If it rains**, we will stay.\n- *If I was you* is often used in speech, but in writing use **If I were you**.\n- *If he would have come* → **If he had come**.\n- *The girl which sings* → **who** sings.\n- Commas missing around a non-defining clause, or added to a defining one."),
    exm("Example 1: choose the type", "Complete: *If I ___ (be) the Minister, I ___ (build) more schools.*",
        ["This is an unreal, imaginary situation: second conditional.", "if + past (were) and would + base verb."], "If I were the Minister, I would build more schools."),
    exm("Example 2: join with a relative clause", "Combine: *Mount Cameroon is the highest peak in the region. It is in the South West.* (non-defining)",
        ["The second sentence is extra information about Mount Cameroon: use *which* with commas.", "Remove the repeated subject *It*."], "Mount Cameroon, which is in the South West, is the highest peak in the region.")],
   [m("Choose the correct sentence.", "If it rains, we will stay at home.", ["If it will rain, we will stay at home.", "If it rained, we will stay at home.", "If it rains, we stayed at home."], "First conditional: if + present, will + verb."),
    m("Complete: *If I ___ you, I would apologise.*", "were", ["am", "will be", "would be"], "Second conditional uses were."),
    t("*Unless you hurry* means the same as *If you do not hurry*.", True, "Unless = if not."),
    m("Which relative pronoun fits? *The woman ___ car was stolen called the police.*", "whose", ["who", "which", "that is"], "Whose shows ownership."),
    m("Third conditional: *If she ___ studied, she would have passed.*", "had", ["has", "would", "was"], "If + past perfect."),
    o("Complete each sentence in your own words:\n1. If it rains tomorrow, ...\n2. If I were the principal, ...\n3. If I had known about the test, ...\nThen write one sentence with a defining relative clause and one with a non-defining clause about a place in Cameroon.",
      "1. If it rains tomorrow, we will cancel the match. 2. If I were the principal, I would build a library. 3. If I had known about the test, I would have revised. The man who sells fish lives near the school. Limbe, which lies on the coast, is known for its beaches.",
      ["1 mark each for the first, second and third conditional with correct tense pattern.", "1 mark: defining clause without commas.", "1 mark: non-defining clause with commas."])],
   [sc("The second conditional is used for...", "unreal or unlikely situations", ["real facts only", "orders", "finished habits"], "Imagined situations."),
    sc("*If + past perfect, would have + participle* is the...", "third conditional", ["zero conditional", "first conditional", "second conditional"], "Past impossible."),
    sc("A non-defining relative clause is written...", "between commas", ["without punctuation", "with a question mark", "in brackets only"], "Commas."),
    sc("The relative pronoun for ownership is...", "whose", ["who", "which", "when"], "Whose."),
    sc("*If you heat water to 100 degrees, it boils* is the...", "zero conditional", ["first conditional", "second conditional", "third conditional"], "General truth.")])

s = p.chapter("ch-reading", "Reading, vocabulary and summary", REF)

LS(s, "comprehension-figurative", "Comprehension: tone, figurative language and implied meaning", 28,
   ["Identify similes, metaphors and personification in a passage.", "Describe the tone and mood of a passage.", "Explain the effect of a figure of speech."],
   [tx("**Harmattan Morning**\nThe harmattan crept down from the north overnight and wrapped Garoua in a dry grey blanket. Dust hung in the air like flour in a bakery, and the sun was a pale coin that nobody could look at for long. Women tied scarves round their heads; the market, usually a river of noise, flowed slowly. Old Mallam Bello, leaning on his stick, laughed at the dust. \"The wind has come to visit us again,\" he said, \"and as usual it has forgotten to wipe its feet.\""),
    ky("definition", "Figures of speech",
       "- **Simile:** compares with *like* or *as*: *dust hung like flour in a bakery.*\n- **Metaphor:** says one thing *is* another: *the sun was a pale coin.*\n- **Personification:** gives human actions to a thing: *the harmattan crept... wrapped Garoua*, *the wind forgot to wipe its feet.*\n- **Hyperbole:** deliberate exaggeration; **irony:** saying the opposite of what is meant.\nAlways name the figure, quote it, and explain its **effect**."),
    ky("definition", "Tone and mood",
       "**Tone** is the writer's or speaker's attitude (humorous, serious, bitter, gentle). **Mood** is the feeling the passage creates in the reader (peaceful, gloomy, tense). In the passage the narrator's tone is **gently humorous**, and Mallam Bello's joke lightens the mood. To find tone, look at the **word choices** and what the characters do."),
    fg(flow("Explaining a figure of speech", ["Quote the words", "Name the figure", "Say what is compared", "Explain the effect on the reader"]),
       "Four steps give full marks.", "A flowchart: quote the words, name the figure, say what is compared, explain the effect on the reader."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- Naming the figure but not explaining its **effect**.\n- Confusing simile (*like, as*) and metaphor (*is*).\n- Describing tone with weak words (*nice, bad*): use *humorous, solemn, ironic*.\n- Taking a metaphor literally: the sun is not really a coin.\n- Quoting too much: choose the key words."),
    exm("Example 1: a simile", "Explain the effect of *Dust hung in the air like flour in a bakery.*",
        ["Figure: a **simile** (like). It compares the dust with fine flour.", "Effect: shows how fine, thick and white-grey the dust is and makes the air seem heavy."], "The simile compares dust with flour, showing how thick and fine the air is."),
    exm("Example 2: personification", "What is the effect of *the wind has forgotten to wipe its feet*?",
        ["Figure: **personification**: the wind is treated as a rude visitor.", "Effect: humour and a friendly tone: Mallam Bello accepts the dust with a smile."], "The personification adds humour and shows Mallam Bello's good-natured attitude.")],
   [m("*The sun was a pale coin* is a...", "metaphor", ["simile", "question", "pun"], "It says the sun is a coin, without like or as."),
    m("*Dust hung in the air like flour* is a...", "simile", ["metaphor", "personification", "irony"], "Like signals a simile."),
    t("The narrator's tone in the passage is angry and bitter.", False, "The tone is gently humorous."),
    m("*The harmattan crept down* is an example of...", "personification", ["hyperbole", "simile", "proverb"], "A wind cannot creep like a person."),
    m("What does Mallam Bello's joke suggest about his character?", "He is good-humoured.", ["He is angry.", "He is afraid.", "He is rude."], "He laughs at the dust."),
    o("In three sentences explain what the writer achieves by describing the market as *usually a river of noise* that now *flowed slowly*. Name the figure of speech.",
      "The writer uses a metaphor, comparing the noisy market to a river. A river suggests movement and sound, so we imagine a busy, loud place. Because it now flows slowly, we understand that the harmattan has made people move and speak less.",
      ["1 mark: metaphor named.", "1 mark: what is compared.", "1 mark: effect on the reader.", "1 mark: link to the harmattan and clear writing."])],
   [sc("A metaphor says...", "one thing is another", ["one thing is like another", "something is not true", "the opposite"], "Direct comparison."),
    sc("The feeling a passage creates in the reader is its...", "mood", ["plot", "setting", "title"], "Mood."),
    sc("To get full marks on a figure of speech, you should also...", "explain the effect", ["copy the line only", "name another author", "count the words"], "Say what effect it has."),
    sc("A gentle, humorous attitude is a type of...", "tone", ["plot", "rhyme", "stanza"], "Tone."),
    sc("Giving human actions to a thing is...", "personification", ["simile", "alliteration", "prefix"], "Personification.")])

LS(s, "summary-intro", "Summary writing: the basics", 28,
   ["Find the main points of a passage.", "Rewrite them in your own words within a word limit.", "Write the summary as one clear paragraph in continuous prose."],
   [tx("A **summary** is a short version of a passage that keeps only the **main points**. It must be in **your own words**, in **continuous prose** (a paragraph, not a list) and within a **word limit**. Summary shows that you understand a text and can express ideas briefly."),
    ky("methode", "The five steps",
       "1. **Read** the passage and the question: *What exactly must be summarised?*\n2. **Underline** the points that answer the question (ignore examples, repetitions, descriptions, direct speech).\n3. **List** the points in note form, in your own words.\n4. **Write** the summary as one paragraph using linking words; stay within the limit.\n5. **Count and check**: grammar, spelling, no copied lines."),
    fg(flow("Summary: from passage to paragraph", ["Read the question and passage", "Underline the main points", "Note them in your own words", "Write one paragraph; count the words"]),
       "Stay in order; do not add your opinion.", "A flowchart of four steps: read the question and passage, underline the main points, note them in your own words, write one paragraph and count the words."),
    ky("methode", "What to leave out",
       "Leave out: **examples** (*for instance...*), **repetitions**, **descriptive detail**, **comments and opinions**, **direct speech**, **figures of speech**. Keep the **key facts**, causes, results and reasons. Use short **substitutes**: *many* for *a large number of*, *because* for *due to the fact that*."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- **Lifting** whole phrases or sentences from the passage.\n- Writing a **list** or bullet points.\n- Going over the **word limit** (marks are lost) or writing far too little.\n- Adding your **own opinion** or new information.\n- Starting with *This passage is about...*: begin directly with the first point."),
    exm("Example 1: shorten a sentence", "Shorten (keep the meaning): *Because the road to our village had been badly damaged by the heavy rains of the past week, the farmers were completely unable to take their cocoa to the market.*",
        ["Identify the core: roads damaged by rain; farmers could not sell cocoa.", "Use shorter words: *The rains damaged the road, so the farmers could not take their cocoa to market.*"], "The rains damaged the road, so farmers could not take their cocoa to market."),
    exm("Example 2: choose the main points", "Which of these is NOT a main point for a summary on *why pupils were late*: (a) the bus broke down (b) the bus was green (c) the driver was ill?",
        ["Main points give **reasons**: (a) breakdown, (c) driver's illness.", "The colour of the bus is a descriptive detail: leave it out."], "(b) The bus was green.")],
   [m("A summary should be written as...", "a continuous paragraph", ["a list", "a table", "a poem"], "Continuous prose."),
    m("Which should you leave out of a summary?", "examples and repetitions", ["main points", "reasons", "key facts"], "Leave out extras."),
    t("Copying sentences from the passage is a good way to write a summary.", False, "Use your own words."),
    m("Which is the best shortening of *due to the fact that*?", "because", ["in spite of", "despite", "however"], "Because has the same meaning."),
    m("The first thing to do when you start a summary is to...", "read the question and passage carefully", ["count your words", "write the conclusion", "copy the first paragraph"], "Know what must be summarised."),
    o("Summarise in about 30 words: *Many young people leave villages for towns in search of work. They hope to find better wages, schools and hospitals. However, jobs are scarce and housing is expensive, so some end up in hard conditions. Meanwhile, the villages lose their strongest workers and the farms suffer.*",
      "Young people move to towns hoping for better wages, schools and hospitals, but jobs are scarce and housing costly, so many suffer, while villages lose their workers and farms decline.",
      ["1 mark: young people move to towns for better services/wages.", "1 mark: jobs scarce, housing expensive.", "1 mark: villages lose workers, farms suffer.", "1 mark: own words, within the limit, continuous prose."])],
   [sc("The word limit in a summary...", "must be respected", ["can be ignored", "is only a guide for others", "applies to the title only"], "Marks are lost beyond it."),
    sc("A summary uses...", "your own words", ["the writer's exact words", "only quotations", "words from the question"], "Own words."),
    sc("Direct speech in the passage is normally...", "left out or changed into a short point", ["copied", "kept entirely", "turned into a poem"], "Remove detail."),
    sc("A summary begins...", "with the first main point", ["with a long introduction", "with your opinion", "with a title"], "Direct start."),
    sc("The best order for the points is...", "the order of the passage", ["random order", "alphabetical", "reversed"], "Keep the logic.")])

LS(s, "vocabulary-register-collocation", "Vocabulary: formal and informal words, collocations and phrasal verbs", 24,
   ["Choose formal or informal words to fit the situation.", "Use common collocations.", "Understand some common phrasal verbs."],
   [tx("The words you choose depend on **who** you write to and **why**. A letter to the principal needs a different **register** (level of formality) from a message to a friend. Learning common word combinations (**collocations**) and phrasal verbs also makes your English sound natural."),
    ky("definition", "Register: formal and informal",
       "Informal: *get, kids, ask for, find out, big, start, help* — Formal: *obtain, children, request, discover, large, commence, assist*.\nFormal writing avoids contractions (*do not*), slang and abbreviations. Informal writing allows contractions and a friendly tone. **Match the register to the reader**: *I would be grateful if you could assist me* (formal) / *Can you help me?* (informal)."),
    ky("definition", "Collocations and phrasal verbs",
       "- **Collocations** are words that go together naturally: *make a mistake* (not *do*), *do homework* (not *make*), *heavy rain* (not *strong rain*), *take a decision* or *make a decision*, *pay attention*.\n- **Phrasal verbs** = verb + particle with a special meaning: *look after* (care for), *give up* (stop), *find out* (discover), *put off* (delay), *turn up* (arrive), *run out of* (have no more)."),
    fg(cols("Informal and formal", ["Informal", "Formal"], [["get", "obtain"], ["ask for", "request"], ["find out", "discover"], ["start", "commence"]], w=420),
       "The same idea, a different register.", "A table with informal words on the left, get, ask for, find out and start, and formal equivalents on the right, obtain, request, discover and commence."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- Slang in an official letter (*Hey Sir, wassup*).\n- Wrong collocation: *do a mistake*, *make homework*, *strong rain*.\n- Splitting phrasal verbs wrongly: *look the word up* is fine, *look up it* is not.\n- Using very formal words in a friendly letter: it sounds cold.\n- Using a phrasal verb literally (*give up* = stop, not hand upwards)."),
    exm("Example 1: raise the register", "Rewrite formally: *Can you give me some info about the exam?*",
        ["*Can you* → *Could you*; *give* → *provide*; *info* → *information*.", "Add courtesy: *please*."], "Could you please provide me with some information about the examination?"),
    exm("Example 2: fix the collocation", "Correct: *She did a big mistake and made her homework late.*",
        ["Mistakes are *made*; homework is *done*.", "*big mistake* is acceptable; *late* needs a better frame: *and was late with her homework*."], "She made a big mistake and did her homework late.")],
   [m("Which word is the most formal?", "commence", ["start", "begin", "get going"], "Commence is formal."),
    m("Choose the right collocation.", "make a mistake", ["do a mistake", "have a mistake", "build a mistake"], "We make mistakes."),
    t("*Give up* means to stop trying.", True, "That is its common meaning."),
    m("Which is correct?", "heavy rain", ["strong rain", "big rain", "high rain"], "Heavy rain is the collocation."),
    m("*We ran out of water* means...", "we had no water left", ["we ran to the water", "we found water", "we sold water"], "Run out of = have no more."),
    o("Rewrite this informal message as a formal note to a teacher: *Hi Sir, I can't come tomorrow cos I'm ill. Can u give me the homework? Thanks.*",
      "Dear Sir, I am unable to attend school tomorrow because I am unwell. I would be grateful if you could provide me with the homework. Thank you. Yours faithfully, Ngu",
      ["1 mark: contractions and abbreviations removed.", "1 mark: formal words (unable, unwell, provide).", "1 mark: polite structure with could/would.", "1 mark: correct greeting and closing."])],
   [sc("Register means...", "the level of formality", ["the rhyme of a poem", "the topic", "the setting"], "Formal or informal."),
    sc("A phrasal verb is...", "a verb plus a particle with a special meaning", ["a long sentence", "a noun group", "a figure of speech"], "e.g. give up."),
    sc("Which is the most formal request?", "Could you kindly assist me?", ["Help me!", "Gimme a hand", "Come on!"], "Polite and formal."),
    sc("Which collocation is correct?", "do homework", ["make homework", "build homework", "take homework"], "We do homework."),
    sc("*Look after* means...", "take care of", ["look at", "look for", "look away"], "Care for.")])

w = p.chapter("ch-writing", "Writing and speaking", REF)

LS(w, "letter-of-application", "Letters of application", 28,
   ["Set out a letter of application.", "Present qualities, skills and experience persuasively.", "Use a polite closing and mention your availability."],
   [tx("A **letter of application** is a formal letter in which you ask for a job, a place or an opportunity: a holiday job, a scholarship, a place in a club. It must convince the reader that you are suitable. A typical task: *Apply for the post of library assistant advertised on the school notice board.*"),
    ky("definition", "Layout and structure",
       "Use the formal layout (your address, date, receiver's address, *Dear Sir/Madam*, subject line, *Yours faithfully*). Structure:\n1. **Paragraph 1:** the post you apply for and where you saw it.\n2. **Paragraph 2:** your **qualifications and qualities** (age, class, results, skills).\n3. **Paragraph 3:** your **experience or interests**, and why you are suitable.\n4. **Paragraph 4:** availability, readiness for an interview, polite thanks."),
    fg(tree("Plan of an application", "Application", [("Post", "where seen"), ("About me", "skills, class"), ("Close", "available, thanks")], h=185),
       "Each paragraph has a job to do.", "A diagram with Application at the top and three arrows to Post (where seen), About me (skills, class) and Close (available, thanks)."),
    ky("methode", "Style",
       "- Be **truthful**, specific and positive: *I have served as library prefect for a year* is better than *I am very good*.\n- Avoid begging (*please, I am very poor*) and boasting.\n- Use formal language and correct spelling.\n- Write **only relevant** information: no family history.\n- Never invent qualifications."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- Forgetting to say **which post** you apply for and **where** you saw the advertisement.\n- Writing the whole life story.\n- Using *Yours sincerely* with *Dear Sir/Madam*.\n- Telling the reader what he must do instead of showing politeness.\n- Leaving out availability or contact information."),
    exm("Example 1: the opening", "Write the opening paragraph of an application for the post of library assistant advertised on the school notice board of GHS Limbe.",
        ["State the post, the source and the purpose.", "*I am writing to apply for the post of library assistant, as advertised on the notice board of Government High School, Limbe, on 3 June.*"], "I am writing to apply for the post of library assistant, as advertised on the notice board of Government High School, Limbe, on 3 June."),
    exm("Example 2: a convincing paragraph", "Write a short paragraph showing that you are suitable.",
        ["Give a quality + proof: responsible (library prefect), organised, reads a lot.", "*I am a Form 3 pupil and have served as library prefect for one year. I am responsible, well organised and I enjoy reading.*"], "I am a Form 3 pupil and have served as library prefect for one year. I am responsible, well organised and I enjoy reading.")],
   [m("What should the first paragraph of an application state?", "the post and where you saw it", ["your family history", "your complaints", "your favourite food"], "Say which post and where you saw it."),
    m("Which closing goes with *Dear Sir/Madam*?", "Yours faithfully,", ["Yours sincerely,", "Cheers,", "Bye,"], "Unnamed receiver."),
    t("In an application you may invent qualifications to impress the reader.", False, "Be truthful."),
    m("Which sentence is the most persuasive?", "I have served as library prefect for one year.", ["I am very good.", "I want the job.", "Please give me the job; I am poor."], "It gives specific proof."),
    m("The last paragraph should usually...", "state availability and thank the reader", ["list your hobbies", "complain", "repeat the address"], "Close politely."),
    o("You are Ngu Tabi, a Form 3 pupil of Government Bilingual High School, Buea. Write a letter (about 150 words) applying for a holiday job as a shop assistant advertised by Mama Ada's shop in Buea Town.",
      "Ngu Tabi, P.O. Box 55, Buea, 10 July 2026\nThe Manager, Mama Ada's Shop, Buea Town\nDear Madam,\nAPPLICATION FOR THE POST OF SHOP ASSISTANT\nI am writing to apply for the holiday post of shop assistant advertised on your shop door on 8 July.\nI am a Form 3 pupil of Government Bilingual High School, Buea. I am honest, hard-working and good at mathematics, and I have helped in my aunt's shop for two holidays, where I served customers and kept the accounts.\nI am available from 20 July to 31 August and can attend an interview at any time. Thank you for considering my application.\nYours faithfully,\nNgu Tabi",
      ["2 marks: layout and closing.", "3 marks: content (post, qualities with proof, availability).", "2 marks: formal language, spelling, punctuation.", "Maximum 7."])],
   [sc("A letter of application is...", "a formal letter", ["a story", "a poem", "a text message"], "Formal."),
    sc("In the second paragraph you should present...", "your qualities and qualifications", ["your complaints", "your fears", "a joke"], "To convince."),
    sc("Which opening is best?", "I am writing to apply for the post of library assistant.", ["Hi, I need a job.", "Give me work.", "Dear friend,"], "States the purpose."),
    sc("You should always...", "be truthful", ["exaggerate", "beg", "copy another letter"], "Honesty."),
    sc("An application ends by...", "thanking the reader and giving availability", ["asking for money", "threatening", "saying goodbye informally"], "Polite close.")])

LS(w, "report-writing", "Writing a report", 28,
   ["Set out a report with a title, headings and recommendations.", "Write in the past tense for events and present for findings, in a formal style.", "Separate facts from opinions."],
   [tx("A **report** gives an organised account of an event, a visit or a problem for a specific reader (the principal, the class, a club). It is **factual, clear and formal**. Typical tasks: *a report on the school sports day*, *a report on the state of the school library*."),
    ky("definition", "Layout and sections",
       "**Heading:** *REPORT ON THE STATE OF THE SCHOOL LIBRARY*; below it: **To / From / Date** (or *Prepared by*).\n1. **Introduction (terms of reference):** purpose and how the information was collected.\n2. **Findings** (body): short paragraphs or numbered points, under headings.\n3. **Conclusion:** a short summary of what the findings show.\n4. **Recommendations:** what should be done, in order of importance.\nSign off with your name and position."),
    fg(flow("Parts of a report", ["Heading: title, To / From / Date", "Introduction: purpose, method", "Findings: facts under headings", "Conclusion and recommendations"]),
       "Use clear headings so the reader finds each part quickly.", "A flowchart of the parts of a report: heading, introduction, findings, conclusion and recommendations."),
    ky("methode", "Style",
       "- **Facts, not feelings**: *Only 40 books were found for 300 pupils* (not *The library is terrible*).\n- Use the **past tense** for what you did or saw and the **present** for conclusions.\n- Use **impersonal, formal** language; short, clear sentences.\n- **Number or bullet** the findings and recommendations.\n- Do not invent details beyond those in the task; keep numbers realistic."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- Writing a **story** or letter instead of a report.\n- No title or headings.\n- Opinions instead of facts, or insulting language.\n- Recommendations that do not follow from the findings.\n- Forgetting the date, the writer or the reader."),
    exm("Example 1: the heading", "Write the heading of a report by a prefect to the principal on the Form 3 clean-up campaign held on 12 May.",
        ["Give a title in capitals, then **To / From / Date**.", "Add the subject clearly."], "REPORT ON THE FORM 3 CLEAN-UP CAMPAIGN HELD ON 12 MAY\nTo: The Principal\nFrom: The Senior Prefect\nDate: 14 May 2026"),
    exm("Example 2: fact or opinion", "Which is suitable for a report: (a) The library is a disgrace. (b) The library has only 40 books for 300 pupils.",
        ["(a) is an opinion expressed emotionally.", "(b) is a factual, measurable statement."], "(b)")],
   [m("A report is mainly...", "a factual, organised account", ["a poem", "a love letter", "a story with dialogue"], "Facts, organised."),
    m("Which belongs in the recommendations section?", "The school should buy 200 more books.", ["The library was untidy.", "I visited on Monday.", "There were 40 books."], "A recommendation says what should be done."),
    t("A report may use headings and numbered points.", True, "They help the reader."),
    m("Which tone is appropriate?", "formal and impersonal", ["slangy", "angry", "playful"], "Reports are formal."),
    m("The conclusion of a report...", "sums up what the findings show", ["introduces new facts", "is a joke", "repeats the date"], "It summarises."),
    o("You are the secretary of the school Environment Club. Write a short report (about 150 words) to the principal on a tree-planting activity held on Saturday 10 May in which 40 pupils planted 60 seedlings near the football field. Include findings and two recommendations.",
      "REPORT ON THE TREE-PLANTING ACTIVITY OF 10 MAY\nTo: The Principal\nFrom: The Secretary, Environment Club\nDate: 12 May 2026\nIntroduction: This report describes the tree-planting activity held on Saturday 10 May near the football field.\nFindings: 1. Forty pupils took part. 2. Sixty seedlings were planted. 3. The soil was dry, so each seedling was watered.\nConclusion: The activity was successful and the pupils worked with enthusiasm.\nRecommendations: 1. The seedlings should be watered twice a week. 2. A fence should be built to protect them.\nSecretary, Environment Club",
      ["2 marks: layout (title, to/from/date, headings).", "3 marks: content (facts given, findings, recommendations).", "2 marks: formal language and punctuation."])],
   [sc("A report starts with...", "a title and To/From/Date", ["a greeting like Dear friend", "a joke", "a poem"], "Layout."),
    sc("Recommendations should...", "follow from the findings", ["be unrelated", "be funny", "be personal attacks"], "Logical."),
    sc("Findings are written in...", "factual language", ["emotional language", "slang", "rhyme"], "Facts."),
    sc("A report is written for...", "a specific reader", ["no one", "anyone at random", "yourself only"], "A named reader."),
    sc("The introduction states...", "the purpose and how information was collected", ["the recommendations", "a story", "a joke"], "Terms of reference.")])

LS(w, "speech-writing", "Writing and giving a speech", 28,
   ["Open and close a speech with the correct forms of address.", "Use persuasive devices: rhetorical questions, repetition and the rule of three.", "Plan a speech for a particular audience."],
   [tx("A **speech** is written to be **heard**. It addresses an audience directly, so it uses a personal, lively style. Typical tasks: *a speech to welcome a guest*, *a speech on the importance of reading*, *a speech for a debate*."),
    ky("definition", "Structure and form of address",
       "1. **Salutation:** *Madam Principal, teachers, ladies and gentlemen, fellow pupils,* (the most important person first).\n2. **Introduction:** greet, say who you are, state the topic.\n3. **Body:** two or three main points, each with a reason or example.\n4. **Conclusion:** summarise, appeal to action, thank the audience: *Thank you for listening.*\nWrite the **title** above: *SPEECH ON THE IMPORTANCE OF READING*."),
    ky("methode", "Persuasive devices",
       "- **Rhetorical question:** *How can we build our country if we do not read?*\n- **Repetition:** *We must read; we must learn; we must lead.*\n- **Rule of three:** *honest, hardworking and brave.*\n- **Direct address:** *you, we, let us...*\n- **Facts and examples** that the audience can check.\nKeep sentences **short and clear** so that listeners can follow."),
    fg(tree("Plan of a speech", "Speech", [("Opening", "greet, topic"), ("Body", "points, proof"), ("Closing", "appeal, thanks")], h=185),
       "The body is where you persuade; the closing is what people remember.", "A diagram with Speech at the top and three arrows to Opening (greet, topic), Body (points, proof) and Closing (appeal, thanks)."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- Forgetting the salutation or the title.\n- Writing it like an essay, with long, complex sentences.\n- Facts that are exaggerated or invented.\n- Only opinions without reasons.\n- Ending abruptly without thanking the audience."),
    exm("Example 1: salutation", "Write a suitable salutation for a speech given to the principal, teachers and pupils at the school assembly.",
        ["The most senior person first, then the others.", "End with a comma."], "Madam Principal, teachers, fellow pupils,"),
    exm("Example 2: a rhetorical question", "Write a rhetorical question for a speech against littering.",
        ["Ask a question whose answer is obvious.", "Link it to the audience: *our*, *we*."], "How can we be proud of our school when our compound is covered in rubbish?")],
   [m("Which salutation is most suitable?", "Madam Principal, teachers and fellow pupils,", ["Hi guys,", "Dear Sir,", "To whom it may concern,"], "Formal address for an assembly."),
    m("*Do we want to be remembered as lazy or as leaders?* is a...", "rhetorical question", ["command", "statement of fact", "title"], "It needs no answer."),
    t("A speech should use very long, complex sentences.", False, "Short, clear sentences work best when listening."),
    m("Which device is the *rule of three*?", "honest, hardworking and brave", ["very very very", "yes or no", "please please"], "Three items in a list."),
    m("A speech normally ends with...", "an appeal and thanks", ["a joke only", "a question mark", "silence"], "Close with an appeal."),
    o("Write a speech of about 200 words to be given at the school assembly on *Why every pupil should read for 30 minutes a day*. Include a salutation, a rhetorical question and a conclusion with thanks.",
      "SPEECH ON READING\nMadam Principal, teachers, fellow pupils,\nGood morning. Today I want to talk about reading. How can we expect to learn if we never open a book?\nReading builds our vocabulary, improves our spelling and opens our minds to the world. A pupil who reads for thirty minutes a day will write better, speak better and understand better. Moreover, a book is cheaper than a ticket to anywhere, yet it can take us everywhere.\nSome will say, 'I have no time.' But we find time for football, for films and for our phones. We can find thirty minutes for a book.\nLet us read: in the morning, in the evening, at home and at school. Thank you for listening.",
      ["2 marks: form (title, salutation, thanks).", "3 marks: content and arguments.", "2 marks: persuasive devices and style.", "1 mark: language."])],
   [sc("The salutation of a speech comes...", "at the beginning", ["at the end", "in the middle", "never"], "At the start."),
    sc("A rhetorical question is...", "asked to make a point", ["asked to get an answer", "a title", "a command"], "No answer needed."),
    sc("A speech is written to be...", "heard", ["read silently only", "drawn", "framed"], "Spoken."),
    sc("Which ending is best?", "I thank you for listening.", ["That is all.", "Bye.", "End."], "Polite and clear."),
    sc("Repetition in a speech is used to...", "give emphasis", ["fill space only", "confuse", "shorten"], "Emphasis.")])

LS(w, "argumentative-essay", "The argumentative essay", 28,
   ["Plan an essay for or against a motion.", "Develop paragraphs with a point, a reason and an example.", "Write a balanced introduction and conclusion."],
   [tx("An **argumentative essay** presents your opinion on a topic and persuades the reader with reasons and examples. A typical title: *Pupils should not be allowed to use mobile phones at school. Discuss.* A good essay is **organised** and **fair**: it considers the other side before explaining why your view is stronger."),
    ky("methode", "Plan in four steps",
       "1. **Introduction:** explain the topic and state your position (thesis) clearly.\n2. **Body:** 2 or 3 paragraphs, each with a **point** (topic sentence), a **reason** and an **example**.\n3. **Counter-argument:** one paragraph on the opposite view and why it is weaker.\n4. **Conclusion:** restate your position in new words and give a final thought."),
    fg(flow("Plan of an argumentative essay", ["Introduction: topic and your position", "Argument 1: point, reason, example", "Argument 2 and the other side", "Conclusion: restate and close"]),
       "Each body paragraph proves one point.", "A flowchart: introduction with topic and position, argument one with point reason and example, argument two with the other side, then a conclusion that restates and closes."),
    ky("methode", "Persuasive language",
       "Useful phrases: *In my opinion..., Firstly, ... Moreover, ... In addition, ... However, some people argue that ... but ..., Therefore, ... In conclusion...* Use **facts and examples** drawn from Cameroonian life. Avoid insulting people and extreme words (*always, never, everyone*)."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- Taking no clear side or changing sides.\n- Reasons with no examples.\n- One long paragraph.\n- Repeating the same point in different words.\n- Emotional, rude or exaggerated language.\n- Introducing new arguments in the conclusion."),
    exm("Example 1: a thesis", "Write a thesis sentence for an essay *Pupils should wear school uniform.*",
        ["Take a clear position and hint at the reasons.", "*I strongly believe that pupils should wear school uniform because it promotes equality, discipline and school pride.*"], "I strongly believe that pupils should wear school uniform because it promotes equality, discipline and school pride."),
    exm("Example 2: point, reason, example", "Develop the point *School uniform promotes equality.*",
        ["Reason: it hides differences in family wealth.", "Example: in one classroom pupils from rich and poor homes look alike."], "School uniform promotes equality. It hides differences in family wealth, so that pupils from richer and poorer homes look alike and no one is laughed at because of his or her clothes.")],
   [m("The sentence that states your position in the introduction is the...", "thesis", ["title", "caption", "moral"], "Thesis."),
    m("Each body paragraph should have...", "a point, a reason and an example", ["only a title", "only questions", "a poem"], "Develop each point."),
    t("A good argumentative essay never mentions the other side.", False, "Mentioning the counter-argument shows balance."),
    m("Which linking word adds another argument?", "Moreover", ["However", "Although", "Unless"], "Moreover adds."),
    m("A conclusion should...", "restate the position and close", ["introduce a new argument", "attack the reader", "repeat the title only"], "Close clearly."),
    o("Write a plan (not the full essay) for: *Farming should be taught in every secondary school. Discuss.* Give the thesis, two arguments with reasons and examples, one counter-argument and the conclusion.",
      "Thesis: Farming should be taught in every secondary school. Argument 1: it teaches useful skills (growing maize, keeping hens; many pupils will farm). Argument 2: it reduces hunger and teaches discipline (school garden can feed the canteen). Counter-argument: some say it takes time from academic subjects, but it can be taught in a few hours a week. Conclusion: farming skills build a strong economy and should be taught.",
      ["1 mark: clear thesis.", "2 marks: two arguments with reasons and examples.", "1 mark: counter-argument and rebuttal.", "1 mark: conclusion."])],
   [sc("A counter-argument is...", "the opposite point of view", ["your main point", "the title", "a summary"], "The other side."),
    sc("The introduction should...", "state the topic and your position", ["list all the examples", "end with thanks", "be left out"], "Clear start."),
    sc("The best link for contrast is...", "however", ["moreover", "firstly", "in addition"], "However contrasts."),
    sc("An essay should be divided into...", "paragraphs", ["one block", "one line", "bullet points only"], "Paragraphs."),
    sc("Which word is too extreme?", "everyone always", ["many", "often", "some"], "Avoid sweeping statements.")])

o_ch = p.chapter("ch-oral", "Oral English", REF)

LS(o_ch, "oral-discussion-debate", "Oral English: discussion, debate and listening", 26,
   ["Take part in a discussion politely.", "Present an argument in a short debate speech.", "Listen for the main points and take notes."],
   [tx("Speaking and listening are tested in class and in oral examinations. In **discussions** and **debates** you give your opinion, listen to others and respond politely. Good speakers are clear, organised and respectful."),
    ky("definition", "Useful expressions",
       "- **Agreeing:** *I agree with you; That is a good point.*\n- **Disagreeing politely:** *I see your point, but...; I am afraid I disagree because...*\n- **Giving an opinion:** *In my view...; I believe that...*\n- **Asking for clarification:** *Could you explain what you mean?*\n- **Interrupting politely:** *Excuse me, may I add something?*\nAvoid shouting, insults and personal attacks."),
    fg(cols("Debate language", ["Purpose", "Example"], [["agree", "That is a good point."], ["disagree", "I see it differently."], ["clarify", "What do you mean?"], ["conclude", "To sum up..."]], w=440),
       "Polite phrases keep a debate friendly.", "A table of debate language: agree, that is a good point; disagree, I see it differently; clarify, what do you mean; conclude, to sum up."),
    ky("methode", "Speaking and listening skills",
       "- **Speaking:** make one clear point at a time, support it with a reason, look at your audience, speak slowly and loudly enough.\n- **Debate speech:** greet the chair and audience (*Mr Chairman, honourable members*), state your side, give two reasons, answer the other side, thank them.\n- **Listening:** listen for **key words**, write short **notes**, and wait before replying."),
    ky("pieges", "Common mistakes",
       "- Interrupting rudely or speaking too long.\n- Attacking the person instead of the idea.\n- Reading a text word for word without looking at the audience.\n- Speaking too fast or too softly.\n- Not listening and repeating what has been said."),
    exm("Example 1: disagree politely", "Someone says *School should start at eight, not seven.* You disagree. What can you say?",
        ["Acknowledge their view first, then give your reason.", "*I see your point, but a seven o'clock start gives us a longer afternoon for sports.*"], "I see your point, but a seven o'clock start gives us a longer afternoon for sports."),
    exm("Example 2: a debate opening", "Write the opening sentences of a speech supporting the motion *Pupils should learn a trade in secondary school.*",
        ["Address the chair and audience, then state your side.", "Mr Chairman, honourable members, I stand firmly in support of the motion."], "Mr Chairman, honourable members, ladies and gentlemen, I stand firmly in support of the motion that pupils should learn a trade in secondary school.")],
   [m("Which is a polite way to disagree?", "I see your point, but I think differently.", ["That is stupid.", "You are wrong.", "Be quiet."], "Polite and respectful."),
    m("A debate speech usually begins by...", "addressing the chair and audience", ["shouting", "reading the conclusion", "leaving the room"], "Salutation first."),
    t("In a discussion you should insult people who disagree.", False, "Respect is required."),
    m("When you listen, you should note...", "key words", ["every word", "nothing", "only the date"], "Key words."),
    m("Which phrase asks for clarification?", "Could you explain what you mean?", ["I agree.", "To conclude,", "Thank you."], "It asks for explanation."),
    o("Write a one-minute speech (about 120 words) for the motion *Mobile phones should be banned from secondary schools*. Choose your side. Include a greeting, two reasons, a reply to the other side and thanks.",
      "Mr Chairman, honourable members, I stand in support of the motion. Firstly, phones distract pupils during lessons. Secondly, they can be used for cheating. Some say phones help research, but the school library and computer room are better places. For these reasons I urge you to support the motion. Thank you.",
      ["1 mark: greeting and thanks.", "2 marks: two reasons.", "1 mark: answer to the opposing view.", "1 mark: clear, polite language."])],
   [sc("In a debate you should...", "attack ideas, not people", ["attack people", "shout", "leave"], "Respect."),
    sc("*I see your point, but...* is used to...", "disagree politely", ["agree completely", "end the debate", "ask a question"], "Polite disagreement."),
    sc("The chairperson of a debate...", "leads the meeting and gives turns", ["wins every debate", "sits silently only", "writes the speeches"], "Leader."),
    sc("A listener should...", "take short notes of key points", ["write every word", "talk at the same time", "sleep"], "Key points."),
    sc("A good speaker...", "speaks clearly and looks at the audience", ["reads without looking up", "mumbles", "speaks very fast"], "Clear delivery.")])

p.write()
